<section xml:id="sec-ws-03-codebook-pass">
  <title>Worksheet 3: Codebook-Assisted Pass</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Apply the frozen working codebook to episodes preserved in a locked blind-pass record.</li>
      <li>Match complete criterion-to-judgment relationships rather than isolated words.</li>
      <li>Distinguish a primary match, alternate matches, OOV language, no match, and unresolved classification.</li>
      <li>Evaluate criterion correctness, inferential validity, and question-goal coverage as separate decisions.</li>
      <li>Preserve the blind extraction while recording every assisted addition, proposed correction, and routing decision.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This worksheet is the third milestone in the researcher training sequence. In the
      blind pass, you recorded the justification visible in each response without access
      to the project codebook or controlled vocabulary. In this milestone, you return to
      that <em>locked</em> record and add the formal analytic decisions that require the
      question-specific codebook and normative frame.
    </p>

    <p>
      The codebook-assisted pass does not replace the blind pass. It creates a second,
      linked layer of analysis. The difference between the two layers is evidence: it
      can show where the codebook clarified a category, where it created pressure to
      reinterpret the response, where the vocabulary is incomplete, or where the
      analytic framework does not yet support a defensible decision.
    </p>

    <p>
      This remains an <term>independent</term> milestone. You may use the approved
      codebook, controlled vocabulary, question frame, and normative reference supplied
      with the packet. You may not inspect another coder's work, an adjudicated answer,
      or a pipeline proposal until this assisted record has been submitted and locked.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-ws-03-status">
      <title>Milestone status</title>
      <p>
        Completing this worksheet does not begin calibration. It establishes whether
        you can apply formal project categories to an independently extracted episode
        while preserving uncertainty, version provenance, and the original blind record.
        Advancement is based on the review described at the end of this worksheet.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-overview">
    <title>Milestone at a Glance</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-overview">
      <title>Purpose and output of the codebook-assisted milestone.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="22%"/>
        <col width="78%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Item</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Description</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Primary purpose</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Test whether the frozen codebook, controlled vocabulary, and question frame can be applied to the locked blind extraction.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Work mode</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Independent and codebook-assisted, but still other-coder-blind and machine-blind.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Starting record</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The approved, locked blind-pass record from Worksheet 2.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Resources added</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The frozen working codebook, controlled vocabulary, question-specific analytic frame, normative reference, and valid-inference set.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Primary deliverable</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A locked assisted record containing code matches, correctness, coverage, status, disposition, confidence, and version provenance.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Next milestone</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Independent calibration, after the research lead confirms that the assisted record is complete and methodologically valid.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-prerequisites">
    <title>Prerequisites and Assigned Reading</title>

    <p>
      Begin only after the research lead has accepted and locked your blind-pass record
      and supplied the approved assisted-pass packet. Review the following material
      before assigning any code:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-codebook-working" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-codebook-entry" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-codebook-oov" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-codebook-criteria" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-codebook-judgments" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-codebook-links" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="sec-codebook-normative-links" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-coding-match" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-coding-correctness" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="subsec-coding-goal-coverage" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="subsec-coding-route" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-decision-matching" text="title"/> and
          <xref ref="subsec-decision-correctness" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-app-codebook-artifacts" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="sec-app-codebook-vocabulary" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="sec-app-codebook-use" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="app-codebook-reference" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-analytic-fields" text="title"/> and
          <xref ref="subsec-app-coding-sheet-provenance-fields" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-matching" text="title"/>,
          <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-correctness" text="title"/>, and
          <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-provenance" text="title"/> in
          <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Read the full definitions, inclusion rules, exclusion rules, examples, and near
      misses for every candidate code you consider. A code name, familiar phrase, or
      keyword is not sufficient evidence for a match.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-access">
    <title>Materials and Access Rules</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-access">
      <title>Resources available and withheld during the codebook-assisted pass.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>You may use</p></cell>
          <cell><p>You must not use</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Your own approved, locked blind-pass record</p></cell>
          <cell><p>An editable replacement that overwrites or silently alters the blind record</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The exact question, representation, goals, and normative reference supplied in the packet</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A different question's codebook or normative frame, even when the wording appears similar</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The assigned frozen working codebook and its full entry definitions</p></cell>
          <cell><p>An older, draft, or unlabeled codebook copy</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The assigned controlled vocabulary and question-specific valid-inference set</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Unapproved synonym lists, personal code mappings, or privately normalized terminology</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The handbook, protocol summary, coding-sheet dictionary, and codebook reference</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Another coder's sheet, notes, classifications, or advice about a response</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The research lead for missing resources, version conflicts, and governing-rule questions</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Pipeline extraction, matching, confidence, disposition, or review output</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>The approved project folder and assigned coding application</p></cell>
          <cell><p>External AI systems, public search, unapproved cloud tools, screenshots, or personal copies of project responses</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-03-preserve-blind">
      <title>Do not retrofit the blind extraction</title>
      <p>
        The purpose of this milestone is not to make the blind record agree with the
        codebook. If the codebook causes you to notice a possible extraction error,
        preserve the original row and record the proposed correction in the assigned
        assisted or review layer. Never edit the locked criterion, link, judgment,
        completeness, or episode boundary in place.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-03-version">
      <title>Use only the assigned versions</title>
      <p>
        At the time this worksheet was drafted, the current frozen shared codebook is
        <c>codebook2-june1</c>. The assignment packet remains authoritative. If the
        packet names a different version, omits a version, or combines artifacts from
        different versions, stop and contact the research lead before coding.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-packet">
    <title>Check the Assisted-Pass Packet</title>

    <p>
      Before adding any assisted annotation, verify that every resource is present and
      versioned. Record the supplied values in the manifest below. The purpose of this
      check is to ensure that every decision can later be interpreted under the exact
      analytic materials available to you.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-packet-manifest">
      <title>Codebook-assisted packet manifest.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="34%"/>
        <col width="66%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Required item</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Value or confirmation</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Batch identifier</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the supplied assisted-pass batch ID.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Locked blind record</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record its file or record identifier and confirm its locked timestamp.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Question identifier(s)</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Confirm that each <c>question_id</c> matches the blind packet.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Response identifiers</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Confirm that every assisted row links to an existing blind <c>response_id</c> and <c>episode_n</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Working codebook</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the exact <c>codebook_version</c> and confirm that the entries are question-appropriate.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Controlled vocabulary</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the exact <c>vocabulary_version</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Question frame</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the <c>question_frame_version</c>, normative reference, goals, and valid-inference set.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Handbook and protocol</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the supplied <c>handbook_version</c> and <c>protocol_version</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Assisted schema</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the sheet, database view, or mapping used to preserve added annotations without altering the blind layer.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-03-stop-packet">
      <title>Stop when provenance is incomplete</title>
      <p>
        Stop and contact the research lead when the blind record is not locked, stable
        identifiers do not align, a governing version is missing, or the codebook and
        question frame appear to concern different questions. Do not repair joins or
        substitute a nearby artifact privately.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-record-layers">
    <title>Preserve the Two Analytic Layers</title>

    <p>
      The assisted record must remain linked to, but distinguishable from, the blind
      record. The physical implementation may use a second worksheet, a normalized
      database table, or versioned review fields. Follow the assigned schema. Whatever
      the implementation, preserve the distinctions below.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-record-layers">
      <title>Required relationship between blind and assisted records.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="27%"/>
        <col width="33%"/>
        <col width="40%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Information</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Blind layer</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Assisted layer</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Screening and episode extraction</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Locked unaided decisions</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Read-only reference; proposed corrections recorded separately</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Codebook match</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Blank</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Primary code, alternate codes, or supported non-match status</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Vocabulary status</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Unfamiliar wording may appear only in notes</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Formal OOV decision and verbatim <c>oov_note</c></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Correctness and coverage</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Blank</p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>c1</c>, <c>c2</c>, episode goal coverage, and response-level missing goals</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Routing</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Blank</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Response status and assigned disposition</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Confidence</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Confidence in unaided screening and extraction</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Confidence in matching, correctness, coverage, and routing</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Provenance</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Blind-pass versions and timestamps</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Assisted-pass versions, timestamps, and link to the blind record</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      When the assigned schema provides review or override fields, use them to record a
      proposed change to the blind extraction. When it does not, use the supplied
      deviation log. Do not create an undocumented parallel convention.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-fields">
    <title>Fields for This Pass</title>

    <p>
      Complete only the fields authorized by the assigned schema. The table below
      describes their research meaning; physical column names may differ when a mapping
      is supplied.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-fields">
      <title>Fields completed, preserved, and withheld during the codebook-assisted pass.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="30%"/>
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Field or field group</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Action</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Assisted-pass rule</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Blind screening and episode fields</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve read-only</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Use as the unit to classify. Record any proposed extraction correction separately.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>code</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete or leave blank</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Enter one strongest accepted code only when the full relationship fits its definition.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>alt_codes</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete when applicable</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record every other defensible code for the same episode. Do not use this field for separate episodes.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>oov_note</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete when applicable</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Copy unfamiliar or unsupported terminology verbatim. Do not translate it silently into preferred language.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>c1</c>, <c>c2</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete when evaluable</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Score complete episodes against the actual problem state and valid-inference set. Leave incomplete or unevaluable episodes blank.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>goal_coverage</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record which question goals the episode's judgment addresses, or the assigned off-target or unclear value.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>missing_goals</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete at response level</p></cell>
          <cell><p>List requested goals not addressed by the response as a whole.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>response_status</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Use the assigned values to preserve ordinary, OOV, no-match, unresolved, or other supported status.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>disposition</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Choose the next-action route defined by the assigned protocol; do not invent a new routing label.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Assisted confidence and notes</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p>State confidence in the assisted decisions and identify the precise source of uncertainty or deviation from the blind record.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>codebook_version</c>, <c>vocabulary_version</c>, <c>question_frame_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record exact labels supplied with the packet.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>coder_id</c>, <c>coded_at</c>, <c>handbook_version</c>, <c>protocol_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve who completed the assisted pass, when, and under which rules.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Other-coder comparison, machine review, disagreement, and adjudication fields</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Leave blank</p></cell>
          <cell><p>These fields belong to later calibration or tool-review work.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-decision-guide">
    <title>Match, OOV, No Match, or Unresolved?</title>

    <p>
      These outcomes describe different analytic conditions. Do not use them as
      interchangeable labels for a difficult response.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-decision-guide">
      <title>Decision guide for assisted classification.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="19%"/>
        <col width="42%"/>
        <col width="39%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Outcome</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Use when</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Primary match</p></cell>
          <cell><p>One code best describes the complete criterion-to-judgment relationship under its definition and boundaries.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Enter that ID in <c>code</c>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Multiple plausible matches</p></cell>
          <cell><p>More than one code defensibly describes the same episode and the current materials do not justify discarding the alternatives.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Enter the strongest candidate in <c>code</c> and the others in <c>alt_codes</c>, with a note.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>OOV</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Relevant terminology or a needed canonical proposition is not represented adequately in the controlled vocabulary.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve the term verbatim in <c>oov_note</c>; record any otherwise defensible code separately.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>No match</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The episode is interpretable with available vocabulary, but no current codebook entry adequately describes its relationship.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Leave <c>code</c> blank and assign the supported no-match status and route.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Unresolved</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Available evidence or current policy does not support one defensible classification among competing possibilities.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve the alternatives, reduced confidence, evidence, and unresolved route.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Incomplete episode</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A criterion or judgment is missing, so there is no complete relationship to match or score.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Do not force a code or correctness pair; preserve the incomplete record and route as assigned.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-procedure">
    <title>Codebook-Assisted Procedure</title>

    <p>
      Process one response at a time. Begin from its locked blind record rather than
      scanning the codebook for familiar language across the batch.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Verify the record.</term> Confirm the stable identifiers, locked blind
          timestamp, and governing artifact versions before adding annotations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Read the full question frame.</term> Review the prompt, representations,
          requested goals, normative mathematical state, and valid-inference set.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Read the locked episode.</term> Consider the complete
          criterion-to-judgment relationship and its recorded link. Do not begin from a
          keyword or code name.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Compare candidate entries.</term> Read the full definition, inclusion
          rule, exclusion rule, positive examples, near misses, and confusable codes for
          every plausible match.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Record matching.</term> Assign a primary code only when supported.
          Preserve defensible alternatives, OOV language, no-match status, or unresolved
          classification instead of forcing certainty.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Evaluate criterion correctness.</term> Set <c>c1</c> from the actual
          mathematical state, not from whether the student's final answer is correct or
          whether the code label sounds appropriate.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Evaluate inferential validity.</term> Set <c>c2</c> from the
          question-specific valid-inference set. A complete episode with an absent link
          receives <c>c2</c>=0; an incomplete episode remains unscored.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Evaluate coverage.</term> Record the goals addressed by each episode's
          judgment and the goals missing from the response as a whole. Keep coverage
          separate from local correctness.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Assign status and disposition.</term> Use the current protocol to route
          ordinary, multiple-match, OOV, no-match, or unresolved cases. Routing records
          the next action; it does not erase the analysis.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Compare without overwriting.</term> If assistance changes your view of
          screening, segmentation, <m>C</m>, <m>I</m>, <m>J</m>, completeness, or
          relation, preserve the blind record and document the proposed change and its
          evidence in the assigned comparison layer.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Record confidence and provenance.</term> Identify the exact source of
          uncertainty and confirm that every governing version is attached to the
          assisted record.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-assignment">
    <title>Assignment</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-manifest">
      <title>Part A: Verify the Analytic Materials</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Complete <xref ref="tab-ws-03-packet-manifest"/> before classifying any
          episode. Stop and report a missing version, broken identifier link, or mismatch
          between the codebook and question frame.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-preserve">
      <title>Part B: Establish the Assisted Record</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Confirm that the assisted record links to the locked blind submission without
          replacing it. For every response and episode, verify the following:
          <ul>
            <li><c>response_id</c>, <c>question_id</c>, and <c>episode_n</c> match;</li>
            <li>the blind extraction is visible as read-only evidence;</li>
            <li>assisted annotations are stored in the assigned layer;</li>
            <li>the blind <c>locked_at</c> value remains preserved; and</li>
            <li>any proposed extraction correction has a separate place to be recorded.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-match">
      <title>Part C: Match Each Episode</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each complete episode:
          <ol>
            <li>identify every plausible codebook entry;</li>
            <li>compare the whole relationship with each entry's definition and boundary rules;</li>
            <li>record the strongest supported code in <c>code</c>;</li>
            <li>record other defensible candidates in <c>alt_codes</c>;</li>
            <li>leave <c>code</c> blank when no current entry is adequate; and</li>
            <li>write a concise evidence-based note for every ambiguous or low-confidence match.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>

        <p>
          A keyword may help locate candidate entries. It may not serve as the final
          rationale for assigning one.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-oov-no-match">
      <title>Part D: Classify Vocabulary and Coverage Gaps</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review every unfamiliar term and every episode left without a primary code.
          Decide whether the case is:
          <ul>
            <li>OOV terminology with an otherwise defensible code;</li>
            <li>OOV terminology that prevents reliable normalization;</li>
            <li>a no-match episode expressible in the current vocabulary;</li>
            <li>an unresolved choice among existing codes;</li>
            <li>an incomplete episode that should not be matched; or</li>
            <li>an ordinary match that merely uses an uncommon surface form.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          Preserve unfamiliar wording verbatim. A proposed new term or code may be noted,
          but it does not become an accepted category during this pass.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-correctness">
      <title>Part E: Evaluate Correctness and Goal Coverage</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each complete and evaluable episode:
          <ol>
            <li>assign <c>c1</c> from the actual truth of the criterion;</li>
            <li>assign <c>c2</c> from the validity of the criterion-to-judgment inference;</li>
            <li>record the question goals addressed by the episode's judgment; and</li>
            <li>record goals missing from the response as a whole.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>

        <p>
          Do not use the final answer to infer either digit. Do not use a
          <m>(1,1)</m> episode to imply complete goal coverage. Leave correctness blank
          for incomplete or genuinely unevaluable episodes.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-route">
      <title>Part F: Assign Status and Disposition</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assign the current response status and disposition using the routing values
          supplied with the packet. Your rationale should identify the condition that
          determines the route, such as:
          <ul>
            <li>ordinary supported match;</li>
            <li>multiple plausible matches;</li>
            <li>terminology review;</li>
            <li>no-match review;</li>
            <li>unresolved analytic decision;</li>
            <li>policy question;</li>
            <li>data-quality problem; or</li>
            <li>privacy or access concern.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          Do not create a new disposition because the available labels feel imperfect.
          Use the closest governing route and document the limitation for review.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-compare">
      <title>Part G: Compare the Assisted and Blind Layers</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each case in which access to the formal materials changes your
          interpretation, record:
          <ol>
            <li>the earliest affected decision;</li>
            <li>the locked blind value;</li>
            <li>the proposed assisted value;</li>
            <li>the codebook, vocabulary, question-frame, or protocol evidence for the change;</li>
            <li>whether the issue reflects coder learning, unclear guidance, or a possible artifact gap; and</li>
            <li>your confidence in the proposed change.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>

        <p>
          This is a comparison record, not permission to rewrite the blind file. A
          change may remain a proposal until review or calibration.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-integrity">
      <title>Part H: Perform the Integrity Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Before submission, confirm that:
          <ul>
            <li>the blind record remains unchanged and linked;</li>
            <li>every code rationale uses the full relationship and code definition;</li>
            <li>alternate matches are preserved rather than hidden;</li>
            <li>OOV and no match are not conflated;</li>
            <li>correctness and goal coverage were evaluated independently;</li>
            <li>all assisted fields carry the correct governing versions;</li>
            <li>no other coder's work or machine proposal influenced the pass; and</li>
            <li>all low-confidence and unresolved decisions contain useful notes.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-03-lock">
      <title>Part I: Lock the Assisted Record</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Submit the assisted record and comparison log through the approved project
          location. Follow the supplied procedure for marking the pass <c>locked</c>.
          After submission, later corrections belong in review, disagreement, or
          adjudication records; they do not replace either the blind or assisted layer.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-questions">
    <title>Questions, Conflicts, and Escalation</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-questions">
      <title>How to handle problems without compromising the assisted pass.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="31%"/>
        <col width="69%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Situation</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Required action</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The codebook version is missing or differs from the packet manifest</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Stop and contact the research lead. Do not substitute another copy.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Two codes fit the same episode</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record a primary candidate and all defensible alternatives, with evidence and reduced confidence when warranted.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>No code fits the episode</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Distinguish no match from OOV, leave the code blank when appropriate, and use the assigned review route.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The codebook suggests the blind extraction may be wrong</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve the blind row and record a proposed assisted correction in the assigned comparison layer.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>The code definition and examples appear inconsistent</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the conflict, avoid forced certainty, and escalate it as a policy or artifact question.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>You want to know how another coder classified the case</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the question and wait for calibration or review.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>You accidentally see another coder's work or pipeline output</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Stop, preserve the work completed before exposure, and report the incident immediately.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>You discover a data-quality, privacy, or access problem</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Stop work and follow the escalation procedure in <xref ref="sec-data-communication" text="title"/>.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Form analytic questions using the evidence structure in
      <xref ref="subsec-data-useful-question" text="title"/>. Identify the exact
      response, decision, competing interpretations, visible evidence, governing rule,
      and action needed. Do not ask only <q>Which code is this?</q>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-deliverables">
    <title>Deliverables</title>

    <p>
      Submit the following through the approved project location:
      <ol>
        <li>the completed assisted-pass packet manifest;</li>
        <li>the unchanged identifier of the locked blind-pass record;</li>
        <li>one assisted annotation for every assigned response and episode;</li>
        <li>primary and alternate matches, OOV or no-match records, and supporting notes;</li>
        <li>criterion and link correctness for every complete evaluable episode;</li>
        <li>episode-level goal coverage and response-level missing goals;</li>
        <li>response status and disposition;</li>
        <li>the assisted-versus-blind comparison log;</li>
        <li>the integrity-check confirmation; and</li>
        <li>the locked assisted record with the version information below.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-03-version-record">
      <title>Version information to preserve with the assisted-pass submission.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="35%"/>
        <col width="65%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Entry</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>worksheet_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>ws-03-codebook-pass-v0.1</c></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>pass_type</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p><c>codebook_assisted</c></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>batch_id</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the identifier supplied with the packet.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>blind_record_id</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the approved identifier or location of the locked blind record.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>blind_locked_at</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Copy the preserved blind-pass lock timestamp.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>handbook_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the version supplied with the assignment.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>protocol_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the version supplied with the assignment.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>codebook_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the exact assigned frozen codebook label.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>vocabulary_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the exact assigned controlled-vocabulary label.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>question_frame_version</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the exact assigned question-frame label.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>coder_id</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Use the assigned project identifier.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>started_at</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record the start of the first assisted work session.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>coded_at</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record completion of the independent assisted pass.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p><c>locked_at</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record when the assisted record was submitted and locked.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-checklist">
    <title>Completion Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before locking the assisted submission, confirm that:
      <ul>
        <li>the packet manifest is complete and all governing artifacts are versioned;</li>
        <li>the assisted record links to an approved locked blind record;</li>
        <li>the blind extraction has not been overwritten or silently revised;</li>
        <li>every complete episode has been compared with the question-specific working codebook;</li>
        <li>every match is based on the full criterion-to-judgment relationship and entry definition;</li>
        <li>alternate plausible matches are preserved;</li>
        <li>OOV, no match, unresolved, and incomplete are used for distinct conditions;</li>
        <li>unfamiliar terminology is copied verbatim rather than silently normalized;</li>
        <li><c>c1</c> evaluates the actual criterion and <c>c2</c> evaluates the inference;</li>
        <li>incomplete episodes remain unscored;</li>
        <li>goal coverage is recorded separately from correctness;</li>
        <li>response status and disposition use the assigned routing values;</li>
        <li>every assisted change in interpretation is recorded without erasing the blind value;</li>
        <li>every low-confidence or unresolved decision contains an evidence-based note;</li>
        <li>no other coder's work or machine proposal influenced the pass;</li>
        <li>all version labels, identifiers, and timestamps are present; and</li>
        <li>the assisted record and comparison log are locked against silent revision.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-03-review">
    <title>Review and Advancement</title>

    <p>
      The research lead first checks procedural integrity: correct artifact versions,
      preserved blind records, complete assisted fields, and compliance with independent
      access rules. Analytic review then examines matching, correctness, coverage,
      status, routing, and any differences between blind and assisted layers.
    </p>

    <p>
      A difference between layers is not automatically an error. The review should ask
      what produced the change:
      <ul>
        <li>the codebook supplied a useful name for the same blind interpretation;</li>
        <li>a definition clarified a legitimate category boundary;</li>
        <li>the codebook anchored the coder toward an unsupported reinterpretation;</li>
        <li>the controlled vocabulary failed to represent the student's language;</li>
        <li>no current code covered an otherwise interpretable episode;</li>
        <li>the question frame clarified correctness or coverage; or</li>
        <li>the current protocol left the decision genuinely unresolved.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The research lead may advance the researcher to independent calibration when the
      assisted record demonstrates that the researcher can:
      <ul>
        <li>preserve the locked blind analysis and record later changes transparently;</li>
        <li>use the correct question-specific and versioned artifacts;</li>
        <li>match complete relationships by definitions rather than keywords;</li>
        <li>preserve alternate plausible codes without collapsing separate episodes;</li>
        <li>distinguish OOV, no match, unresolved, and incomplete cases;</li>
        <li>evaluate criterion correctness and inferential validity independently;</li>
        <li>separate local correctness from question-goal coverage;</li>
        <li>assign status and disposition using the governing protocol;</li>
        <li>write useful evidence-based rationales and uncertainty notes; and</li>
        <li>complete the pass without other-coder or machine anchoring.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Possible outcomes are:
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title>Advance</title>
          <p>The assisted record is procedurally valid and demonstrates defensible use of the formal analytic artifacts.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Targeted matching remediation</title>
          <p>Complete a short new set focused on confusable codes, multiple matches, OOV, or no-match decisions.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Targeted correctness or coverage remediation</title>
          <p>Complete a short new set focused on <c>c1</c>/<c>c2</c> independence or local-versus-global evaluation.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Repeat the assisted pass</title>
          <p>Complete a fresh packet when independence was compromised, the blind record was altered, or several assisted decisions remain unstable.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Resolve an artifact problem</title>
          <p>Repeat affected items when the codebook, vocabulary, question frame, protocol, or identifier mapping was incomplete or inconsistent.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      Review corrections must be stored in the assigned review record. The locked blind
      and assisted submissions remain unchanged. Their differences will become useful
      evidence during calibration and later evaluation of codebook or pipeline effects.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>